\newlabel{eq:deriv-std-example}{{5.1}{29}{The Standard Derivative-Based Parser}{equation.5.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The direct construction uses two passes. The forward pass derives the input using \texttt  {deriv} (Figure~\ref {fig:deriv-rust}). The backward pass walks the derivation sequence in reverse, using $\mathit  {mkEps}$ (Figure~\ref {fig:mkeps-rust}) to build a parse tree for the empty word from the final nullable (Figure~\ref {fig:nullable-rust}) expression. Then $\mathit  {inj}$ (Figure~\ref {fig:inject-rust}) reinserts one consumed character at a time, moving through $w$ right to left. After injecting $b$, the tree is $(\mathit  {Right}((),b),\mathit  {Right}())$, with $()$ marking the position still to be filled. Injecting $a$ replaces that position by the subtree $\mathit  {Right}(a,b)$, giving the complete tree $(\mathit  {Right}(a,b),\mathit  {Right}())$. }}{29}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:two-pass}{{5.1}{29}{The direct construction uses two passes. The forward pass derives the input using \texttt {deriv} (Figure~\ref {fig:deriv-rust}). The backward pass walks the derivation sequence in reverse, using $\mathit {mkEps}$ (Figure~\ref {fig:mkeps-rust}) to build a parse tree for the empty word from the final nullable (Figure~\ref {fig:nullable-rust}) expression. Then $\mathit {inj}$ (Figure~\ref {fig:inject-rust}) reinserts one consumed character at a time, moving through $w$ right to left. After injecting $b$, the tree is $(\mathit {Right}((),b),\mathit {Right}())$, with $()$ marking the position still to be filled. Injecting $a$ replaces that position by the subtree $\mathit {Right}(a,b)$, giving the complete tree $(\mathit {Right}(a,b),\mathit {Right}())$}{figure.caption.18}{}}
\citation{sulzmannlu2014}
\citation{sulzmannlu2014}
